# TicketX backend environment.
#
# Copy to backend/.env for local development:
#
#     cp backend/.env.example backend/.env
#
# backend/.env is gitignored. config.py loads it on startup with load_dotenv(),
# which does NOT override variables that are already set in the environment, so a
# real variable in your shell or your host's settings always wins.
#
# In production this file does not exist. Set these as real environment
# variables in your host's dashboard instead (for Render: the service's
# Environment section).

# --- Database -------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave unset locally to use the repo-root ticketx.db. In production, point this
# at PostgreSQL. Render and Neon URLs work as-is; database.py normalizes
# postgres:// and postgresql:// to the installed psycopg 3 driver.
# DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///./ticketx.db
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/database

# --- Auth -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Change both of these before exposing the app to anything but localhost.
# TICKETX_SECRET=change-me
# TICKETX_PASSWORD_SALT=change-me

# --- Google login (optional) ----------------------------------------------
# Without this, password login keeps working and the Google button stays
# hidden. Must match VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID in frontend/.env.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# --- Event suggestions (optional) -----------------------------------------
# Groq writes the one-line "why we picked this" copy. The ranking itself is
# computed in recommend.py and never depends on a model, so leaving this blank
# degrades to rule-based copy instead of breaking the app.
# GROQ_API_KEY=

# Model ids are configuration, not constants: providers retire model ids on
# their own schedule, and a retired one fails with 404. The fallback is tried
# once on a 404 before the feature gives up and shows rule-based copy.
# GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b
# GROQ_FALLBACK_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b
# GROQ_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=8

# --- Seat holds (optional) -------------------------------------------------
# How long a seat hold survives while the attendee fills in their details.
# Seconds, default 600 (ten minutes). Values outside 1..3600 fall back to 600.
# SEAT_HOLD_SECONDS=600

# --- Demo data (optional) -------------------------------------------------
# Set to 1/true/yes/on to create the demo events, registrations, and a
# "demo / demo123" account at startup. Idempotent, so restarts will not duplicate
# rows. Or skip this and run the seeder by hand instead:
#
#     python backend/scripts/seed_demo.py
#
# PREVIEW AND DEMO USE ONLY. This creates an account with a fixed, publicly
# documented password. Do not enable it on a deployment that real people hold
# accounts on.
# TICKETX_SEED_DEMO=1
